Reject a codec_bit_depth outside {10, 12} instead of corrupting the heap.

xeve's internal sample type is 16-bit unconditionally -- `typedef s16 pel`
(src_base/xeve_port.h:52). Its reconstructed and reference picture buffers,
however, are sized from param->codec_bit_depth: xeve_set_init_param() stores it
as ctx->pa.bit_depth (src_base/xeve_enc.c:1706), the picture allocator hands it
to xeve_picbuf_alloc(), which builds a colour space with
XEVE_CS_SET(cf, bit_depth, 0), and xeve_imgb_create() then takes the plane
stride from XEVE_CS_GET_BYTE_DEPTH(cs) -- which is 1 for an 8-bit depth.

So at codec_bit_depth=8 every picture plane is allocated one byte per sample and
subsequently written as 16-bit pels. The encoder runs off the end of every such
allocation. ASan localizes it precisely, on a 352x288 encode:

  WRITE of size 640 at ... 144 bytes before a 368640-byte region
    #1 picbuf_expand
    #2 xeve_picbuf_expand
    #3 xeve_pic_finish
    #4 xeve_enc
  allocated by xeve_imgb_create

640 is one padded row (stride 384+2*144 at one byte per sample), 368640 is
640*576 -- i.e. the plane really was allocated at byte depth 1 -- and 144 is
PIC_PAD_SIZE_L (MAX_CU_SIZE+16), the left/top pad that picbuf_expand writes into
before the buffer even begins.

THIS IS REACHABLE FROM UNMODIFIED UPSTREAM, not just from an embedding. xeve's
own CLI takes --codec-bit-depth as a documented option (README.md:245) and
v0.5.1's xeve_app segfaults on it:

  $ xeve_app -i in.yuv -w 352 -h 288 -z 25 --codec-bit-depth 8 -o out.evc
  Segmentation fault (exit 139)

NOTE FOR ANYONE RE-VERIFYING VIA THAT CLI: it still segfaults after this patch,
and that is NOT this patch failing. The library now refuses the value correctly
-- xeve_create() returns -101 -- but the app's own error path then walks two
uninitialized stack arrays: app/xeve_app.c:933-934 declare
IMGB_LIST ilist_org[MAX_BUMP_FRM_CNT] / ilist_rec[...] with no initializer, and
the ERR: label at :1441-1442 calls imgb_list_free() on both, which dereferences
whatever was on the stack. ANY early xeve_create() failure crashes xeve_app that
way, bit depth or not. That is an independent upstream defect in the application,
not the library, and nothing this wrap ships reaches it -- we install the archive
and link FFmpeg against it. Verify this fix from a library-level caller, where
xeve_create() cleanly returns XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT.

It matters for THIS project because FFmpeg reaches the same field. libxeve.c
forwards arbitrary -xeve-params through xeve_param_parse() (ffmpeg-8.0.1
libavcodec/libxeve.c:360-361) and codec_bit_depth is in that parse table
(src_base/xeve_param_parse.c:95), so
`ffmpeg -c:v libxeve -xeve-params codec-bit-depth=8` corrupts the heap in the
binary we ship.

WHY THE CHECK GOES HERE, and not in xeve_param_check(). That function is the
obvious home -- it is the library's declared parameter validator -- but putting
it there would fix nothing for FFmpeg, because FFmpeg calls it BEFORE it applies
the user's -xeve-params:

  libxeve.c:353   xeve_param_check(&cdsc->param)     <- validates the defaults
  libxeve.c:361   xeve_param_parse(&cdsc->param,...) <- then applies user values
  libxeve.c:370   xeve_create(cdsc, NULL)

xeve_set_init_param() is downstream of all three: it is called from
xeve_create() and so sees the final parameter set no matter which route filled
it. It is also shared by both profiles -- src_main's xevem_set_init_param()
(src_main/xevem_util.c:3037-3040) delegates to this base version -- so one check
covers the MAIN build this wrap produces and the BASE build alike. And it is
where upstream already keeps exactly this kind of guard: the function opens with
a "/* check input parameters */" block of xeve_assert_rv(...,
XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT) tests on w/h, qp, keyint and threads. This is one
more of those, in the same style.

The alternative fix -- allocating the picture buffers at two bytes per sample so
that 8-bit coding actually works -- is deliberately NOT taken. Upstream
documents the supported set as "codec internal bit depth (10, 12)"; 8 is out of
contract, the path is plainly never exercised, and making it function would mean
adopting a code path upstream does not test. Rejecting the value closes the
memory-safety hole, which is the part that affects what we ship, with a diff
small enough to be obviously correct.

Behaviour change is confined to inputs that previously corrupted memory:
codec_bit_depth 10 (the default) and 12 are untouched; anything else now returns
XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT (-101) from xeve_create() instead of scribbling past
every picture plane. Verified both directions against this wrap's build: a
352x288 8-frame encode at 10-bit still produces 8413 bytes over 8 output frames
and is clean under ASan, while the same probe at 8-bit now fails at
xeve_create() with -101 and no ASan report.

Upstream status: NOT fixed. v0.5.1 (2024-06-24) is the latest tag and the one
this recipe pins, and xeve_param_check() on master still contains no bit-depth
validation of any kind (checked 2026-08-02). Applied with -f --fuzz=0 and a
reverse dry-run guard (the recipes/ffmpeg.sh idiom) rather than the lenient
"|| true" form, because a security patch that silently stops applying is the one
failure mode that must not pass quietly.

The meson port of this project carries the identical patch as
subprojects/packagefiles/patches/xeve-reject-unsupported-codec-bit-depth.patch.

--- a/src_base/xeve_enc.c
+++ b/src_base/xeve_enc.c
@@ -2281,6 +2281,18 @@
     xeve_assert_rv(param->qp >= MIN_QUANT && param->qp <= MAX_QUANT, XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT);
     xeve_assert_rv(param->keyint >= 0 ,XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT);
     xeve_assert_rv(param->threads <= XEVE_MAX_THREADS ,XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT);
+    /* The encoder's internal sample type (pel) is s16 unconditionally
+       (src_base/xeve_port.h:52), but the reconstructed/reference picture buffers
+       are sized from this field: ctx->pa.bit_depth = param->codec_bit_depth
+       (:1706) reaches xeve_picbuf_alloc(), which builds a colour space with
+       XEVE_CS_SET(..., bit_depth, 0) and lets xeve_imgb_create() take the plane
+       stride from XEVE_CS_GET_BYTE_DEPTH() -- 1 byte per sample at 8-bit. The
+       encoder then writes those planes as 16-bit pels and runs off the end of
+       every allocation. Only 10 and 12 are supported (README.md: "codec internal
+       bit depth (10, 12)"), so reject anything else here rather than corrupt the
+       heap. */
+    xeve_assert_rv(param->codec_bit_depth == 10 || param->codec_bit_depth == 12,
+                   XEVE_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT);
 
     if(param->disable_hgop == 0)
     {
